```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is entirely on pc_0_0 and is limited to its default gateway
configuration. During the reported "workstation maintenance task",
pc_0_0's default route was changed from the correct gateway (10.0.0.1,
leaf_router_0_0's eth2 interface) to 10.0.0.254, an address that does
not exist anywhere in the fabric.

The mechanism is straightforward: traffic destined for any prefix outside
10.0.0.0/24 must be forwarded to the default gateway. With the gateway
set to 10.0.0.254, pc_0_0 issues ARP requests for that address, receives
no reply (confirmed: ARP state for 10.0.0.254 is FAILED/incomplete in the
neighbor table), and the kernel immediately returns "Destination Host
Unreachable" for every off-subnet packet. Because 10.0.0.1 is on the
same /24 as pc_0_0, it is reachable at Layer 2 without consulting the
default route — which is why that single address still responds to ping.

Nothing is wrong in the routing fabric. leaf_router_0_0, all spine and
super-spine routers, and pc_0_1 are functioning normally; BGP sessions
are established and routes are being correctly advertised and forwarded.
The fault is entirely local to pc_0_0's IP stack.

Root cause: the default route on pc_0_0 was misconfigured to point at the
non-existent gateway 10.0.0.254 instead of 10.0.0.1.

Corrective fix: on pc_0_0, delete the bad default route and restore the
correct one:
  ip route del default
  ip route add default via 10.0.0.1 dev eth0
To make it persistent, update /etc/network/interfaces (or the relevant
netplan/NetworkManager config) to set gateway 10.0.0.1.
=== END ===
```